  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 225 PM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A beautiful start to the weekend so far today with high \npressure positioned over the region. The clear skies, light winds, \nand seasonable temperatures will continue through tonight. Expect \nanother round of fairly heavy frost by Sunday morning, with lows in \nthe low 20s to low 30s. Tomorrow will be a near repeat of today, \nthough south flow will increase a bit later in the day as the ridge \naxis shifts east of the region. Relative humidity values will once \nagain drop to around 30% tomorrow afternoon, and highs will be \na little warmer than today, mainly in the low to mid 60s. \n\nThe south flow will continue Sunday night ahead of a cold front, \nhelping to keep temperatures quite a bit warmer than the past couple \nof nights, especially in the Champlain/St Lawrence Valleys. The \nfront will push southward out of Canada and through our area on \nMonday, bringing mainly increasing clouds as it does so. A few light \nrain showers can't be ruled out, mainly over the higher terrain in \nthe northern mountains. However, given recent trends and the very \ndry antecedent airmass, expect most areas will see only sprinkles, \nif anything. Winds will turn to the northwest behind the front, \nushering in another cool and dry airmass. We'll see a return to much \ncooler temperatures; Tuesday will likely only get into the 40s to \nlow 50s in most areas after morning lows in the 20s to around 30F.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A strong area of high pressure will build in overhead \nearly Wednesday behind our Tuesday cold front helping to extend our \ndry period for many. Southerly flow associated with the high will \nalso help to rebound our temperatures into the late week with highs \nwarming from the the low to mid 50s Wednesday to the upper 50s to \nmid 60s by Friday. There is another chance for a weak frontal \npassage late week, but it looks rather unimpressive and unable to \nbreak us from this dry pattern. Of note in the ensemblistic \nguidance, both the GEFS and euro ensembles show only a 30-40% chance \nfor a cumulative inch of rain through the first week of November. \nWhile the prolonged stretch of dry weather will continue with some \ndays of low relative humidity values next week, prevailing wind \nconditions look too light to note any serious fire weather concerns \nright now. That said, channeled southerly flow Thursday afternoon \ncould see trends towards marginal critical winds with gusts in the \nforecast between 20 to 25 MPH. It is still to far out to say with \nany certainty, but Thursday winds will need to be monitored going \nforward for any fire weather concerns.",
